WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:12.870
Let s talk about classic chrome

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:18.390
Recently I made 2 new sony film simulations,
one is a replica after the fujifilm classic

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:22.910
chrome film simulation, and the other one
is a picture profile made after the original

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:27.230
kodachrome 64 film stock,
and in this video I will explain in detail

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:32.650
the main characteristics and differences between
the Fujifilm and the Sony Classic Chrome

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:36.590
Let me know in the comments if you want to
find out more about the Kodachrome 64 film

00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:38.640
simulation and I ll make another video about
it

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.949
Before we start, remember, all sony film simulation
recipes can be found on my website

00:00:43.949 --> 00:00:46.090
Let s start with the classic chrome

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:50.260
this sony picture profile was made after the
fuji film simulation and I used the fuji xs10

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:52.699
to compare the 2 sample clips

00:00:52.699 --> 00:00:56.629
As you can see, they look very similar with
some slight differences

00:00:56.629 --> 00:01:01.210
They are identical in terms of contrast, fuji
seems to have a better dynamic range but with

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:06.740
worse noise performance and in temrs of color
it s about 90% matching, which is a pretty

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:11.480
good result taking into consideration I was
able to do this in camera only, with the use

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:13.920
of picture profile settings.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.570
As you can see the sony is much much cleaner
and has more details than the fuji, which

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:22.330
seems pretty muddy.
Because of the light gathering capabilities

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:27.790
of the sony a7 III, the sensor capture about
a stop more light than the fuji, so I had

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:32.730
to lower the exposure by one stop to match
the brightness in the 2 cameras, which again

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:36.660
will make the sony much cleaner and has a
clear advantage here.

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:42.080
Unfortunately, with the cine1 Gamma on Sony,
the Dynamic range seems tp be lower than the

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:46.500
fuji at first glance,
but one thing to remember is that the cine1

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:50.720
gamma is a lowlight profile, and has 109%
output,

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:55.740
which means you can recover 9% of the burnt
highlights. And i ll show you exactly what

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.240
i m talkin about

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:01.130
as you can see in this image, it seems like
the highlights are completely blown out but

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:05.070
if we start recovering the highlights and
the shadows, we can see that, it actually

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:09.450
contains more detail and has a higher dynamic
range than how it initially seemed like,,

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:14.480
and remember , this is only 8 bit,, on 10
bit cameras you will have a better color rendition

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:24.380
and even less noise.

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:29.070
You could set the profile to Cine4 to increase
the Dynamic Range inside the camera, but the

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:34.000
Noise Performance lowers considerably and
the colors change a bit, especially the yellows

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:40.260
shift towards green, and skintones towards
pink, which for me is a big nono

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:44.670
In theory the cine4 skintones can be easily
corrected in camera just by lowering the color

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:49.981
phase to let s say -2, that will shift the
reds a little more towards orange, but that

00:02:49.981 --> 00:02:55.920
will change the whole color spectrum, shifting
the yellows even closer to green, and I honestly

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:57.560
just don t like that for a look.

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:01.750
From my experience, the yellow is the most
problematic of all colors, especially on the

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:02.750
sony s

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:07.290
that s why some companies, like canon, choose
to desaturate their yellows inside the camera

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:13.330
to render Perfect Skintones.
Ahh, fun fact, yellow doesn t even exist,

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:17.690
atleast not how we see it.
The human eye can only capture colors of red

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:21.840
blue and green, yellows and magentas are actually
generated by the brain.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:26.900
If you don t mind the color shifting of the
cine4, you can definitely use it instead of

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:32.580
Cine1 for Higher Dynamic Range, but only in
well lit conditions. In lowlight situations,

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:37.200
I find the cine4 to be quite noisy without
applying some noise reduction.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:40.740
So besides that, let s take a closer look
at the colors

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:44.819
The sony reds are shifted towards pink, while
fuji is more orange

00:03:44.819 --> 00:03:48.800
The sony greens are more olive while fuji
greens are shifted towards cyan

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:53.130
And the yellows are very close, fuji being
shifted towards orange while sony looking

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:54.290
more accurate to real life

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:58.180
besides that, the rest of the colors are more
or less identical

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:02.020
Overall I would rate this profile 90% identical
to the fujifilm,

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:06.560
and you know, we were really paying close
attention to the color differences, but when

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:10.630
you shoot in a real life situations it might
not even be that obvious, but you still get

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:12.209
really nice colors out of camera!

00:04:12.209 --> 00:04:17.780
but still let's bring it even closer to the
Fujifilm I'll show you exactly how to further

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:20.620
adjust it it in davinci resolve for a perfect
match

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:26.759
so in the color tab in the hue vs hue menu,
shift the reds towards Orange,

00:04:26.759 --> 00:04:30.610
next take the greens and shift them a little
bit towards Cyan

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:53.439
now drag the yellows just a little bit towards
Orange

00:04:53.439 --> 00:05:02.080
and finally, shift the magentas

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:08.889
now let's see the final result
it's almost indistinguishable

00:05:08.889 --> 00:05:13.129
to save you some time I will upload this lut
on my website so you can download it for free

00:05:13.129 --> 00:05:16.770
So that s pretty much all I wanted to show
you about the classic chrome

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:21.639
let s move on to the kodachrome 64, a film
stock that is more interesting to me

00:05:21.639 --> 00:05:27.029
and that is because replicating the kodachrome
64 was, without any doubt, my most difficult

00:05:27.029 --> 00:05:30.539
and biggest challenge of replicating any film
stock

00:05:30.539 --> 00:05:34.460
let me know in the comments if you want me
to talk about the kodachrome 64 sony film

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:36.400
simulation
and i ll make a video about it!

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:38.440
Till next time, have fun and take care
cya!

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:39.440
Rather than going for the fujifilm kodachrome
simulation, which, for me, did not seems to

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:40.440
look anything like the original kodachrome,
so I decided to go on a different route

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:41.440
Disclaimer
Compared to digital, Film is an organic medium,

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:42.440
which means that results vary even if the
image has been shot same place with same film

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:43.440
stock. It has many more factors that influence
the final rendition of the color. So if you

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:44.440
develop your pictures in 2 places they might
come out totally differently, and that s what

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:45.440
we love about film, the unexpected.
With my film simulation recipes, I am trying

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:46.440
to bring back a little bit of that unexpected
and infuse digital medium with a feeling of

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:47.440
novelty anf discovery. These film simulations
are just an rejuvenation attempt of film and

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:48.440
will not be 100% accurate. That being said,
i hope you will enjoy this video and let s

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:49.440
get started

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:50.440
This film stock is just so complex and volatille,
it s really hard to find 2 pictures that look

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:51.440
alike on the internet

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:52.440
and the fact it s not being produced anymore
it s not making it any easier

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:53.440
The creation of this picture profile alone,
took me about 2 months and at one point I

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:54.440
almost gave up because of the complexity of
the project.

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:55.440
Funny thing is I accidentally created the
kodak ultramax400 in the process of creating

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:56.440
the kodachrome film simulation

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:57.440
so for 2 months I kept searching and gathering
sample photos of kodachrome 64, in hopes of

00:05:57.440 --> 00:05:58.440
putting them all together and finding the
characteristic of the film, the so called

00:05:58.440 --> 00:05:59.440
pattern or what makes this film so unique

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:00.440
and it was almost impossible to find the same
characteristics in 2 photos, they all looked

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:01.440
like being shot on totally different film
stocks

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:02.440
Asked people on forums and everybody seems
to have a different memory of how kodachrome

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:03.440
looked, the only thing in common, was that
kodachrome was the best film ever made, and

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:04.440
they all loved it!

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:05.440
And finally, after comparing hundreds of pictures,
the only 2 characteristics that I could see

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:06.440
appearing in each photo, was the blueish magenta
tones and the accurate greens, so I went for

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:07.440
that

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:08.440
The whole process was veru frustrating, continuosly
going back and forths , at oone point I think

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:09.440
I had 5 ore 6 different kodachrome recipes,
shooting and testing them all in the same

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:10.440
time to see which one is the best
it was totall madness

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:11.440
That was the point where I almost gave up

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:12.440
Also that was the moment god was like i m
going to give this dude a break , and after

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:13.440
searching some more kodachrome shots, I found
a forum post with 2 images taken in the same

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:14.440
place, same lighting conditions, one on kodachrome
and the other on digital

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:15.440
Wow, now, not only I can finally see an accurate
depiction of kodachrome64 with almost the

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:16.440
whole spectrum of colors, but I can also compare
it to digital and see how the film interpreted

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:17.440
colors, more exactly how are the colors shifted.

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:18.440
Now I can understand why everybody loves kodachrome
and how it gained its reputation

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:19.440
I want to thank his name for posting these
pictures. Definitely helped in creating the

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:20.440
profile

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:21.440
Comparing these 2 shots right off the bat
we can see the dominance of the velvety blues

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:22.440
in the sky,

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:23.440
this is a consequence of the specific developing
process kodachrome had. This film had about

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:24.440
7 chemicals to go through for its development,
very complicated indeed,

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:25.440
and this velvety blues are a consequence of
poor chemical handling

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:26.440
If we continue analyziing the images we can
see how the yellow seems shifted towards green

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:27.440
and desaturated, while reds are heavily shifted
towards pink, being super deep and desaturated.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:28.440
There is a slight green hue in the brightest
part of the clouds

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:29.440
This part interests me most, can you see the
blueish tones that appear in the hills? Interestingly

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:30.440
enough, they are yellow in the digital shot,
so I am wondering if the terrain changed in

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:31.440
time or it s just how the film sees the colors?

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:32.440
Now I think you can understand why replicating
this film stock was my biggest film simulation

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:33.440
challenge up until now, and i m going to be
honest with you from the start,

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:34.440
with the settings presented in the picture
profile menu, we can only do so much getting

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:35.440
close to the same colors of the kodachrome
64, we need a little bit of post processing

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:36.440
for this look

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:37.440
So the profile I made will be a really good
base giving fairly accurate colors to the

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:38.440
kodachrome, and the fine adjustments will
be done in post processing with the help of

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:39.440
the luts

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:40.440
The luts are called Neochrome Luts the classics,
and it includes multiple looks for the newer

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:41.440
sony film simulations, like the kodachrome,
kodak ultramax400 fuji superia, classic chrome,

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:42.440
classic neg etc

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:43.440
you can find the luts and settings for the
film simulation recipe on my website

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:44.440
If you ever shot kodachrome and think this
profile can be improved please leave a comment

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:45.440
with some feedback and I will try to bring
it closer

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:46.440
Your opinion really does matter and it s making
this community better, so please let us know

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:47.440
what you think

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:48.440
Try all the new sony film simulations
And till next time, take care and have fun

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:48.442
shooting
Cya

